B.aay=new A.c1(B.bV,["Operating Systems","What is the primary difference between a process and a thread?","A process is an executing program with its own dedicated memory address space, file descriptors, and system resources. A thread is a lightweight unit of execution within a process that shares the parent process's memory space, code, and data sections while maintaining its own program counter, registers, and stack."],t.D)
B.aaA=new A.c1(B.bV,["Operating Systems","What are Coffman's four necessary conditions for deadlock to occur?","The four Coffman conditions are:\n1. Mutual Exclusion: At least one resource must be held in a non-shareable mode.\n2. Hold and Wait: A process holds at least one resource while waiting to acquire additional resources held by other processes.\n3. No Preemption: Resources cannot be forcibly taken from a process holding them; they must be released voluntarily.\n4. Circular Wait: A closed chain of processes exists where each process holds resources needed by the next process in the chain."],t.D)
B.aaF=new A.c1(B.bV,["Operating Systems","How does virtual memory paging work, and what is a page fault?","Virtual memory divides logical memory into fixed-size blocks called pages, and physical memory into corresponding blocks called page frames. The Memory Management Unit (MMU) translates logical page addresses to physical frame addresses via a page table. A page fault is a hardware interrupt triggered when an executing program accesses a page that is not currently mapped into physical RAM, causing the OS to retrieve it from secondary storage (swap/paging space)."],t.D)
B.aaJ=new A.c1(B.bV,["Operating Systems","What is the difference between a binary semaphore and a mutex?","A mutex (mutual exclusion lock) has ownership semantics: only the thread that locked the mutex can unlock it. A binary semaphore is a signaling mechanism with an integer value (0 or 1) that can be signaled (posted/released) by any thread, not just the thread that acquired (waited on) it."],t.D)
B.aax=new A.c1(B.bV,["Database Management","Explain the ACID properties in database transaction management.",'ACID stands for:\n\u2022 Atomicity: An entire transaction completes successfully, or all its changes are completely rolled back ("all or nothing").\n\u2022 Consistency: Transactions transition the database from one valid state to another, preserving all schema constraints.\n\u2022 Isolation: Concurrent execution of transactions yields the same state as if they were executed serially.\n\u2022 Durability: Once a transaction commits, its changes survive system crashes or power failures.'],t.D)
B.aaE=new A.c1(B.bV,["Database Management","What are 1NF, 2NF, and 3NF in relational database normalization?","\u2022 1NF (First Normal Form): All column values must be atomic (no repeating groups or arrays) and each row must be uniquely identifiable.\n\u2022 2NF (Second Normal Form): Meets 1NF, and all non-key attributes are fully functionally dependent on the entire primary key (no partial key dependencies).\n\u2022 3NF (Third Normal Form): Meets 2NF, and no non-key attribute is transitively dependent on the primary key (no transitive dependencies)."],t.D)
B.aaI=new A.c1(B.bV,["Database Management","What is the difference between a Primary Key and a Unique Key constraint?","A Primary Key uniquely identifies each row in a table, cannot contain NULL values, and each table can have at most one primary key. A Unique Key enforces uniqueness across rows for specified columns, can permit one or more NULL values (depending on the SQL dialect), and a table can possess multiple unique key constraints."],t.D)
B.aaN=new A.c1(B.bV,["Database Management","What is a B-Tree index, and why is it preferred for relational database lookups?","A B-Tree (balanced tree) index is a self-balancing search tree data structure that maintains sorted data and allows searches, sequential access, insertions, and deletions in logarithmic time (O(log n)). It minimizes disk I/O reads by grouping multiple keys per tree node, matching operating system page block sizes."],t.D)
B.aaC=new A.c1(B.bV,["Computer Networks","Compare the 7-layer OSI Reference Model with the 4-layer TCP/IP Model.","\u2022 OSI Model (Theoretical): Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application.\n\u2022 TCP/IP Model (Practical implementation):\n  1. Network Access (combines Physical & Data Link)\n  2. Internet (corresponds to Network layer; IP, ICMP)\n  3. Transport (TCP, UDP)\n  4. Application (combines Session, Presentation, Application; HTTP, DNS, SSH, FTP)."],t.D)
B.aaz=new A.c1(B.bV,["Computer Networks","Describe the TCP Three-Way Handshake process for establishing a connection.","TCP connection establishment uses three segments:\n1. SYN: The client generates an Initial Sequence Number (ISN_c) and sends a segment with SYN flag set to the server.\n2. SYN-ACK: The server receives SYN, responds with its own ISN_s, and acknowledges the client with ACK = ISN_c + 1.\n3. ACK: The client sends an ACK segment with ACK = ISN_s + 1. The connection is now established and data transmission begins."],t.D)
B.aaP=new A.c1(B.bV,["Computer Networks","How does Domain Name System (DNS) resolution resolve a domain to an IP address?","When a user queries a domain name:\n1. The client checks local browser cache and OS hosts cache.\n2. If missed, it queries the recursive DNS resolver (ISP or 8.8.8.8).\n3. The recursive resolver asks a Root Nameserver (which directs to the TLD nameserver, e.g. .com).\n4. The TLD nameserver directs to the domain's Authoritative Nameserver.\n5. The authoritative nameserver returns the IP address (A/AAAA record) to the resolver, which caches and returns it to the client."],t.D)
B.aaO=new A.c1(B.bV,["Computer Networks","What are the key technical differences between IPv4 and IPv6?","\u2022 Address Space: IPv4 uses 32-bit addresses (~4.3 billion addresses in dotted decimal notation, e.g., 192.168.1.1). IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses (~3.4 x 10^38 addresses in hexadecimal notation separated by colons).\n\u2022 Header Complexity: IPv6 has a streamlined 40-byte fixed header without checksums, accelerating router packet processing.\n\u2022 Configuration: IPv6 supports Stateless Address Autoconfiguration (SLAAC) and native IPSec encryption."],t.D)
B.aaD=new A.c1(B.bV,["Mathematics","What are eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a square matrix A?","An eigenvector v of a square matrix A is a non-zero vector that, when multiplied by A, yields a scalar multiple of itself: A*v = \u03bb*v. The scalar \u03bb is known as the eigenvalue corresponding to eigenvector v. Eigenvalues are computed by solving the characteristic equation: det(A - \u03bb*I) = 0."],t.D)
B.aaQ=new A.c1(B.bV,["Mathematics","State Bayes' Theorem and explain each component term.","Bayes' Theorem states: P(A|B) = [P(B|A) * P(A)] / P(B)\n\u2022 P(A|B): Posterior probability of event A occurring given evidence B has occurred.\n\u2022 P(B|A): Likelihood of observing evidence B given hypothesis A is true.\n\u2022 P(A): Prior probability of hypothesis A before observing evidence.\n\u2022 P(B): Marginal probability of observing evidence B."],t.D)
B.aaK=new A.c1(B.bV,["Mathematics","What is the rank of a matrix and how is it related to the nullity?","The rank of a matrix is the maximum number of linearly independent row or column vectors in the matrix, indicating the dimension of its column/row space. According to the Rank-Nullity Theorem for an m x n matrix representing linear transformation T: Rank(T) + Nullity(T) = n (the number of columns / dimension of the domain)."],t.D)
B.aaM=new A.c1(B.bV,["Mathematics","What does the Central Limit Theorem (CLT) state in probability and statistics?","The Central Limit Theorem states that the distribution of sample means of independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) random variables approaches a normal (Gaussian) distribution as sample size n increases (typically n >= 30), regardless of the shape of the underlying population distribution, provided the population has a finite variance."],t.D)
B.aaL=new A.c1(B.bV,["Physics","Explain the Photoelectric Effect and state Einstein's Photoelectric Equation.","The photoelectric effect is the emission of electrons from a metal surface when electromagnetic radiation of sufficiently high frequency (above the threshold frequency f_0) strikes it. Einstein's photoelectric equation is: E_k(max) = h*f - \u03a6, where E_k(max) is the maximum kinetic energy of emitted photoelectrons, h is Planck's constant, f is incident light frequency, and \u03a6 (h*f_0) is the metal work function."],t.D)
B.aaG=new A.c1(B.bV,["Physics","State Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle and its physical implications.","Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle states that it is fundamentally impossible to simultaneously determine both the exact position (x) and exact momentum (p) of a quantum particle with arbitrary precision: \u0394x * \u0394p >= \u0127 / 2, where \u0127 = h / (2*\u03c0). This is not an experimental measurement limitation, but an inherent wave-particle property of quantum systems."],t.D)
B.aaH=new A.c1(B.bV,["Physics","What is Gauss's Law in electrostatics and what is its integral form?","Gauss's Law states that the total electric flux \u03a6_E through any closed Gaussian surface is equal to the net charge Q_enclosed enclosed within the surface divided by the electric permittivity of free space \u03b5_0: \u222e E \xb7 dA = Q_enclosed / \u03b5_0. It provides an efficient method to calculate electric fields in symmetrically charged systems."],t.D)
B.aaB=new A.c1(B.bV,["Physics","Summarize the four fundamental Maxwell's Equations of electromagnetism.","The four Maxwell's equations describe how electric and magnetic fields interact with matter and each other:\n1. Gauss's Law for Electricity: \u2207 \xb7 E = \u03c1 / \u03b5_0 (Electric charges generate diverging electric fields).\n2. Gauss's Law for Magnetism: \u2207 \xb7 B = 0 (No magnetic monopoles exist).\n3. Faraday's Law of Induction: \u2207 \xd7 E = -\u2202B/\u2202t (Time-varying magnetic fields produce circulating electric fields).\n4. Ampere-Maxwell Law: \u2207 \xd7 B = \u03bc_0*J + \u03bc_0*\u03b5_0*(\u2202E/\u2202t) (Electric currents and changing electric fields produce magnetic fields)."],t.D)
